  "live_records": {
    "q-276645": {
      "dom_id": "step-q276645",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{(4x+7)^{2}}$ is defined for $x>-\\frac{7}{4}.$ Find ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{(4x+7)^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{(4y+7)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{(4y+7)^{2}} \\\\ x(4y+7)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (4y+7)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{(4y+7)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{x}} \\\\ |4y+7| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 4y+7 & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 4y & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that for $y>-\\frac{7}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n|4y+7|=4y+7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
