      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{6x+2}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$  \n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{6x+2}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{6y+2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{6y+2} \\\\ x(6y+2) & =5 \\\\ 6xy+2x & =5 \\\\ 6xy & =5-2x \\\\ y & =\\frac{5-2x}{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{5-2x}{6x}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
