    "q-276631": {
      "dom_id": "step-q276631",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{7-5x}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$  \n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{7-5x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{7-5y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{7-5y} \\\\ x(7-5y) & =2 \\\\ 7x-5xy & =2 \\\\ -5xy & =2-7x \\\\ 5xy & =7x-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7x-2}{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{7x-2}{5x}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
