    "q-148507": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148507",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{x-8}{5x+1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{x-8}{5x+1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{y-8}{5y+1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{y-8}{5y+1} \\\\ -x & =\\frac{y-8}{5y+1} \\\\ -x(5y+1) & =y-8 \\\\ -5xy-x & =y-8 \\\\ 8-x & =5xy+y \\\\ 8-x & =y(5x+1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{8-x}{5x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{8-x}{5x+1}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
