    "q-148508": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148508",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5-2x}{x+1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5-2x}{x+1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5-2y}{y+1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5-2y}{y+1} \\\\ x(y+1) & =5-2y \\\\ xy+x & =5-2y \\\\ xy+2y & =5-x \\\\ y(x+2) & =5-x \\\\ y & =\\frac{5-x}{x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{5-x}{x+2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
