    "q-148584": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148584",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "The function $f(x),$ defined as \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2x+k}{x-1},\\,x\\ne 1\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-8}{x-2},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2x+k}{x-1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2y+k}{y-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2y+k}{y-1} \\\\ x(y-1) & =2y+k \\\\ xy-x & =2y+k \\\\ xy-2y & =x+k \\\\ y(x-2) & =x+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{x+k}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+k}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-8.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-148584-a",
