    "q-148537": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148537",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{7x+k}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1+3x}{7x}.$ What is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{7x+k}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{7y+k}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{7y+k} \\\\ x(7y+k) & =1 \\\\ 7xy+kx & =1 \\\\ 7xy & =1-kx \\\\ y & =\\frac{1-kx}{7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1-kx}{7x}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
