    "q-148539": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148539",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "The function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{kx-2}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3+2x}{5x}.$ What is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{kx-2}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{ky-2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{ky-2} \\\\ x(ky-2) & =3 \\\\ kxy-2x & =3 \\\\ kxy & =3+2x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3+2x}{kx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3+2x}{kx}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
