    "q-148588": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148588",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "The function $f(x),$ defined as \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3-kx}{x+1},\\,x\\ne -1\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3-x}{x+4},\\,x\\ne -4.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3-kx}{x+1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3-ky}{y+1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3-ky}{y+1} \\\\ x(y+1) & =3-ky \\\\ xy+x & =3-ky \\\\ xy+ky & =3-x \\\\ y(x+k) & =3-x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3-x}{x+k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3-x}{x+k}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
